<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kabars</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kabars were three tribes that rebelled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> in the 9th century and joined the Hungarians. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>, they formed the vanguard in war and taught the Hungarians the Khazar language. They took part in the Hungarian conquest of the Carpathian Basin around 895 and were later absorbed into the Hungarian population.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kabars, also called Qavars or Khavars, were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> rebels. Their rising against the Khazar Khaganate in the early 9th century was notable enough to be recorded by Constantine Porphyrogenitus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a>. According to the historian Magocsi, a violent civil war in Khazaria in the 820s ended in their defeat, and the losers fled in two directions: northward to the Varangian Rus' in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a>, and southward to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>, who had formerly been loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Khazars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Those who joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> went with the confederacy called Hét-Magyar, the seven Hungarians, from Levedia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etelkoz">Etelköz</a>, while others under Khan-Tuvan sought refuge with the Rus'. Magocsi argues that Kabar refugees among the Varangian traders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> raised the traders' prestige, so that by the 830s a new power centre, the Rus' Khaganate, had come into being. The name Kiabar on the Kievian Letter suggests that some Kabars settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> as well.</p><p>Known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Cowari, the Kabars served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> as vanguard and rearguard. In 894 the Byzantine emperor Leo VI, at war with Simeon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, called in the Hungarians, who under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and attacked Bulgaria; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> answered by setting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> on their rear. Arnulf, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, also sought Hungarian help against Svatopluk of Great Moravia. The Kabars took part in the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and the forming of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and after Great Moravia collapsed the Hungarians settled in Hungary for good in 907.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A Turkic aristocracy among the Hungarians may explain why Byzantine protocol under Constantine Porphyrogenitus always called the Hungarian rulers Princes of the Turks. The religion of the Kabars is debated. At least part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> elite converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, while most people of the khaganate stayed pagan. Since the conversion was begun by the ruler, scholars have doubted that rebels against him would have followed it, and the Kabars may have joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> before it took place. One theory links graves with Jewish symbols at Čelarevo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> to them, and the remains and linguistic traces ascribed to them are likewise disputed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kabar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Three <b>Kabar</b> tribes, rebels from the Khazars, joined them.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-tribal-federation">Hungarian tribal federation</a></li></ul></section>
